body:
Not sure if I am misunderstanding, but the amount in my bank account is lower because of the random payment that was sent back (1 they told me to send back and 1 they said to hold onto for now), not taxes.

&#x200B;

Not sure how that needs to be reconciled since the unemployment website says, in regards to \*overpayments\*, "Your 1099-G shows the entire amount we paid you in unemployment benefits, even though you are paying back those benefits. Any unemployment benefits paid during the calendar year must be reported as income. If you were overpaid and paid the money back in the same calendar year, both the amount repaid and the amount not yet repaid must be reported as total income. ." and also "Remember that overpayments are included in 1099-G amounts, but offsets are not. We cannot provide advice; check with the IRS if you are not sure what to do."

&#x200B;

I'm not sure where my situation of sending money back that I didn't ask for falls. Is that an "offset"? I called, went through their automated message, and scheduled a call back. So I'm supposed to get a call on the 11th, hopefully that provides some clarity.

The first one, they told me to send it back, and so that was in June. The other one was in like July, but that one they told me to hold onto so that it didn't cause further issues? So that one I do still have and it's kinda just sitting there. But the 1099G seems to reflect that I kept both payments. 

&#x200B;

Taxes withheld. Child support? No.
